ere  israel is criticized for pursuing  foolish policies or israel is criticized  in ways that make it clear that american  and israeli interests are sometimes at  odds with each other then you begin to  question the special relationship right  you begin to say why are we supporting  israel in an unqualified way when the  interests of the two countries are at  odds  so the lobby does not want people like  john mearsheim and steve walt saying  that israel and the united states often  have different interests  because that might undermine the special  relationship  instead what the lobby wants is for  everyone to sort of be on the same page  and say that israel is an indispensable  ally and there's virtually nothing that  israel ever does that is wrong and  therefore the united states should  support israel and the argument that  steve and i make in the book is that  from israel's point of view this is  actually a foolish policy certainly a  foolish policy from america's point of  view but israel we argue is a normal  country like every other country in the  world israel sometimes pursues smart  policies and sometimes it pursues  foolish policies we have never seen a  country in the history of the world that  got it right every time  so our view is that when israel gets it  wrong we ought to be able to criticize  israel just like we criticize the united  states when it gets it wrong but the  special relationship prohibits you from  doing that  and to maintain that rather unusual  relationship which again is not healthy  for the united  that there's no st  of israel and this is i think what  explains in good part why there's hardly  any criticism of israel in the  mainstream media was there any spinning  or lying in 1948 when harry truman  recognized  israel  no i mean  there was significant pressure brought  to bear on harry truman this is well  documented to recognize  israel immediately and to help  get the united nations general assembly  accept  the partition of p